{"id":"matrix-factorization-sgd-step","t":"Matrix factorisation: one SGD epoch with biases","tags":["Recommender Systems","Optimization"],"d":"Medium","rt":"numpy","fn":"mf_epoch","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"Explicit-feedback matrix factorisation predicts $\\hat r_{ui} = \\mu + b_u + b_i + p_u \\cdot q_i$.\n\nWrite `mf_epoch(ratings, mu, bu, bi, P, Q, lr, reg)` that runs one pass of SGD over `ratings`, a list of `(u, i, r)` triples, **in the given order**. For each triple, with error $e = r - \\hat r_{ui}$ computed from the current parameters:\n\n- $b_u \\mathrel{+}= \\eta(e - \\lambda b_u)$, $b_i \\mathrel{+}= \\eta(e - \\lambda b_i)$\n- $p_u \\mathrel{+}= \\eta(e\\, q_i - \\lambda p_u)$ and $q_i \\mathrel{+}= \\eta(e\\, p_u - \\lambda q_i)$, both using the values of $p_u$ and $q_i$ from **before** this update.\n\n`mu` stays fixed. Do not modify the inputs. Return `(bu, bi, P, Q, rmse)` where `rmse` is the root mean squared error over `ratings` **after** the epoch.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef mf_epoch(ratings, mu, bu, bi, P, Q, lr, reg):\n    \"\"\"Return updated (bu, bi, P, Q, rmse).\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["Copy P[u] and Q[i] before updating either, or the second update sees the new value of the first.","np.array(x, float) makes a copy, so the caller's arrays stay unchanged."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef mf_epoch(ratings, mu, bu, bi, P, Q, lr, reg):\n    bu, bi = np.array(bu, float), np.array(bi, float)\n    P, Q = np.array(P, float), np.array(Q, float)\n    for u, i, r in ratings:\n        e = r - (mu + bu[u] + bi[i] + P[u] @ Q[i])\n        bu[u] += lr * (e - reg * bu[u])\n        bi[i] += lr * (e - reg * bi[i])\n        pu, qi = P[u].copy(), Q[i].copy()\n        P[u] += lr * (e * qi - reg * pu)\n        Q[i] += lr * (e * pu - reg * qi)\n    err = [r - (mu + bu[u] + bi[i] + P[u] @ Q[i]) for u, i, r in ratings]\n    return bu, bi, P, Q, float(np.sqrt(np.mean(np.square(err))))\n","ex_md":"## Why biases first\nMost of the variance in ratings is \"this user rates high\" and \"this film is liked\". The bias terms absorb that so the latent factors only model interaction effects. This was one of the larger single gains in the Netflix Prize models.\n\n## The simultaneous update\nThe gradient of the squared error with respect to $p_u$ involves $q_i$ and vice versa. Updating $p_u$ first and then using the new value for $q_i$ is a different (and slightly wrong) step; with large learning rates it can oscillate.\n\n## SGD versus ALS\nSGD is simple and handles ratings one at a time. Alternating least squares fixes one side and solves the other exactly, which parallelises well and is the usual choice for implicit feedback where every missing cell is a weak negative.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- How would you handle a new user with no ratings?","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"[(0, 0, 5.0)]\n3.0\n[0.0]\n[0.0]\n[[0.1, 0.2]]\n[[0.3, -0.1]]\n0.1\n0.0","out":"(array([0.199]), array([0.199]), array([[0.1597, 0.1801]]), array([[ 0.3199, -0.0602]]), 1.5617539900000001)"},{"in":"[(1, 2, 4.0), (3, 0, 1.0), (3, 4, 2.0), (1, 4, 3.0), (1, 4, 2.0), (1, 3, 3.0), (0, 0, 5.0), (3, 4, 3.0), (3, 1, 3.0), (3, 0, 2.0), (0, 2, 5.0), (0, 1, 3.0)]\n3.5\narray([0., 0., 0., 0.])\narray([0., 0., 0., 0., 0.])\narray([[-0.07819085, -0.02571922],\n       [ 0.00081422, -0.02756029],\n       [ 0.12940638,  0.10067243],\n       [-0.27111625, -0.18890132]])\narray([[-0.01747721, -0.04221904],\n       [ 0.0213643 ,  0.02173219],\n       [ 0.21178388, -0.11120208],\n       [-0.0377605 ,  0.20427716],\n       [ 0.0646703 ,  0.06630634]])\n0.05\n0.02","out":"(array([ 0.11971371, -0.08959545,  0.        , -0.27443583]), array([-0.10636562, -0.04631707,  0.09516539, -0.02119557, -0.16566505]), array([[-0.06254162, -0.03577076],\n       [-0.00083039, -0.04189695],\n       [ 0.12940638,  0.10067243],\n       [-0.27330723, -0.18660474]]), array([[ 0.02719991, -0.00879448],\n       [ 0.02726371,  0.02554198],\n       [ 0.20598032, -0.1135767